A||||3||JohnnyK||How to bench bleed master cylinder (clutch)||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||09-04-2000||02:10 AM||||I have read through old threads, but no one mentions how to do this (they all just do it, and apparently I should know how).. Anyways, I want to start here, and then replace the steel lines, so how would I go about doing this? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||William Federle||09-04-2000||08:46 AM||||I put in a new clutch master cylinder when I was making my 1984 Fiero clutch work. I didn't know about bench-bleeding so I just installed it (that was a big project in itself) and bled the whole clutch system with my wife's help. She pushed the clutch and I worked the bleed valve. This was all after replacing the aluminum clutch pedal with a steel one. The last step after I didn't get anything but a few tiny bubbles out of the main clutch line was to unscrew the bleed valve, cover the hole with a piece of plastic, grab the slave cylinder shaft with a pair of channel locks and push it back into the cylinder. I held it there while my wife re-inserted the bleed valve and tightened it. My clutch has worked ever since.<P>I'm sorry this is so long, but it's the whole story - the things that were difficult for me were the new clutch pedal installation and the new master cylinder installation. I don't know what bench bleeding accomplishes, but I don't see how it would have shortened this process.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||09-04-2000||02:03 PM||||Thats what a lot of people are telling me. As far as pushing the plunger on the slave goes, I ran into a problem. Even with the reservoir almost overflowing, whenever I pushed the plunger in, the fluid in the master would be sucked bone dry. I finally had to fix this by having someone pour fluid in while I pushed the plunger. Hopefully someone else reads this, because it looks like this may be useful in diagnosing my problem..||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||09-04-2000||03:17 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>...whenever I pushed the (slave) plunger in, the fluid in the master would be sucked bone dry.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ummmm.... this doesn't make any sense at all. This is NOT possible.<P>If the reservoir is being sucked dry when you push in and then <I>release</I> the slave plunger, then that's a different story.<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 09-04-2000).]||||reg||5
